📘 TOPIC SHEET 1: Search Types
(Relevance vs Categorized vs Quick Find vs Dataverse Search)
12 questions in exam — HIGH priority ❌ Your weak area
________________________________________
📖 Concept
Microsoft Power Platform has 4 search types. The exam LOVES testing which one to use:
Search Type	What It Does	Key Strength	Limitation
Dataverse Search (formerly Relevance Search)	Searches across ALL tables enabled for search, uses Azure Search index	✅ Inflectional matching ("running" → "run"), searches Notes & Attachments, fuzzy matching	Must be enabled by admin, only indexed columns
Categorized Search	Searches within one table at a time, exact keyword match	✅ Always available, organized by table tabs	❌ NO inflectional matching, ❌ does NOT search Notes/Attachments
Quick Find	Searches within the current view using Quick Find columns	✅ Fast, works on current view	❌ Only searches columns added to Quick Find view, ❌ NO inflectional
Advanced Find	Filter-based query builder, complex conditions (AND/OR)	✅ Multiple conditions, precise filtering	❌ NOT a keyword search, it's a filter tool
🧠 The Critical Difference (exam trick)
The word "running" is stored in a Note:
•	Search for "run": 
o	✅ Dataverse Search → FINDS it (inflectional: run → running ✅, searches Notes ✅)
o	❌ Categorized Search → FAILS (no inflectional matching ❌)
o	❌ Quick Find → FAILS (no inflectional, doesn't search Notes ❌)
________________________________________
🔑 Rules to Memorize
Rule	Remember
1	Dataverse Search = the ONLY one with inflectional matching
2	Dataverse Search = the ONLY one that searches Notes & Attachments
3	Categorized Search = searches by exact keyword, table by table
4	Quick Find = searches ONLY columns configured in the Quick Find View
5	Advanced Find = NOT a search, it's a filter/query builder
6	Search inside a model-driven app = Dataverse Search or Categorized
7	Search outside an app (across M365) = Microsoft Search
8	Dataverse Search must be enabled by admin in environment settings
________________________________________
📝 All Related Questions + Answers
Q36-37: Categorized Search for "run" in Notes?
Scenario: User created a Note with "running" on a Contact. Can't find it.
Solution: Use Categorized Search for "run"
Answer: ❌ No — Categorized Search does NOT support inflectional matching and does NOT search Notes
Why others wrong: Categorized = exact keyword only, no Note/Attachment search
________________________________________
Q38: Relevance Search for "run" in Notes?
Same scenario
Solution: Use Relevance Search for "run"
Answer: ✅ Yes — Relevance Search supports inflectional matching (run → running) AND searches Notes
Key: This is the #1 search question in the exam
________________________________________
Q39: Quick Find for "run" in Notes list?
Same scenario
Solution: Quick Find on the Notes list
Answer: ❌ No — Quick Find only searches configured columns, no inflectional matching
________________________________________
Q58: Dataverse Search for "run"?
Same scenario
Solution: Dataverse Search for "run"
Answer: ❌ No (at the time of this question, Dataverse Search was different from Relevance Search)
⚠️ Updated: In current versions, Dataverse Search = Relevance Search (renamed). So the answer may now be Yes — see Q251
________________________________________
Q251: Dataverse Search for "run"? (Updated version)
Same scenario
Answer: ✅ Yes — Microsoft renamed Relevance Search to Dataverse Search. Now it DOES find inflectional matches in Notes
Exam tip: If the exam says "Dataverse Search" in a recent question → treat it as Relevance Search
________________________________________
Q293-294: Dataverse Search for "run"? (Another variant)
Answer: ❌ No in this version (older question)
Lesson: The exam may have CONFLICTING answers depending on when the question was written. Know BOTH versions
________________________________________
Q63-64: Bicycle repair — two search requirements
Requirement 1: Search for customers with bicycle type "Contoso" AND tires "Fords" → Advanced Find (multiple filter conditions)
Requirement 2: Search all tables for any record containing "broken" → Relevance/Dataverse Search (cross-table keyword search)
Why: Advanced Find = filter builder (AND/OR conditions). Relevance = keyword across all tables
________________________________________
Q81-82: Search inside app vs outside app
Requirement 1: Search data inside the app → Dataverse Search
Requirement 2: Search data outside the app → Microsoft Search
Key: Microsoft Search = searches across M365 (SharePoint, Teams, Outlook + Dataverse)
________________________________________
Q96: Prevent filtering on a custom field in Advanced Find
Answer: Set the Searchable property to No on the Field Properties form
Not: Delete the column, not business rules, not Quick Find columns
Key: The "Searchable" property controls whether a field appears in Advanced Find filters
________________________________________
🔧 Apply Section — Verify It Yourself
✅ Exercise 1: See Dataverse Search in Action
1.	Go to https://make.powerapps.com → select your environment
2.	Open any model-driven app (e.g., Customer Service Hub)
3.	Look at the search bar at the top → this is Dataverse Search
4.	Type a partial word (e.g., "run") → see if it finds inflectional matches
5.	If no results → Admin may need to enable it: 
o	Power Platform Admin Center → Environment → Settings → Product → Features → Dataverse Search = ON
✅ Exercise 2: Compare Categorized vs Dataverse Search
1.	In the same app, click the search bar
2.	If Dataverse Search is OFF → you'll see Categorized Search (table tabs at top)
3.	If Dataverse Search is ON → you'll see a unified search bar (no table tabs initially)
4.	Notice: Categorized = tabs per table. Dataverse = unified results
✅ Exercise 3: Quick Find View Configuration
1.	Go to make.powerapps.com → Tables → select Contact
2.	Click Views → find "Quick Find Active Contacts"
3.	Open it → see which Find columns are configured (right panel)
4.	Only THESE columns are searched when using Quick Find
5.	Try adding/removing a column → see how it affects search
✅ Exercise 4: Advanced Find (Filter Builder)
1.	Open any model-driven app
2.	Click the funnel icon (🔍) or go to Advanced Find (may be in Settings)
3.	Build a filter: e.g., Account Name contains "Contoso" AND City equals "Amman"
4.	Notice: This is a FILTER, not a keyword search — you build conditions
✅ Exercise 5: Searchable Property
1.	Go to make.powerapps.com → Tables → Account → Columns
2.	Click any column → expand Advanced options
3.	Find "Searchable" toggle → this controls if the field appears in Advanced Find
4.	Set it to No → the field disappears from Advanced Find filters
