📘 TOPIC SHEET 3: Business Rule Scope
(Entity/Table vs All Forms vs Specific Form)
~10 questions — repeatedly tested ⚠️
________________________________________
📖 Concept
Business rules have a Scope that controls WHERE they execute:
Scope	Where It Runs	Use Case
Table (formerly Entity)	Server-side — works in Canvas apps, Model-driven, API, import	✅ Required for Canvas apps
All Forms	Client-side — ALL model-driven app forms for that table	Model-driven: same rule on every form
Specific Form (e.g., "Account Form")	Client-side — ONLY that one form	Model-driven: different rules per form
🧠 The Exam Trick
App Type	Required Scope	Why
Canvas App	Table (Entity)	Canvas apps don't use model-driven forms — only server-side (Table) scope works
Model-driven (all forms)	All Forms	Applies to every form in every model-driven app
Model-driven (one app only)	Specific Form	Only applies to the selected form
⚠️ Canvas App Limitations
Business rules in Canvas apps (Table scope) can ONLY:
•	✅ Validate data and show error messages
•	✅ Set field values
•	✅ Set field requirement levels (required/optional)
They CANNOT:
•	❌ Show or hide columns
•	❌ Enable or disable columns
•	❌ Create recommendations
________________________________________
🔑 Rules to Memorize
Rule	Remember
1	Canvas app → Table scope (ALWAYS)
2	Model-driven, all forms → All Forms scope
3	Model-driven, specific app only → Specific Form scope
4	Canvas apps: NO show/hide, NO enable/disable, NO recommendations
5	Table scope = server-side = works everywhere (API, import, canvas, model-driven)
6	All Forms / Specific Form = client-side = model-driven only
________________________________________
📝 All Related Questions + Answers
Q10-11: Business rule for canvas app — which scope?
Answer: ✅ B. Entity (Table)
Why: Canvas apps require Table/Entity scope because they don't use model-driven forms
❌ Screen1 — not a valid scope
❌ All Forms — model-driven only
❌ Global — not a valid scope
________________________________________
Q125-126: Two business rules, different apps
•	Canvas app: set Business Type to "Large" if currency > X → Table scope
•	Model-driven app: recommend re-evaluation when credit hold, this app only → Specific Form scope
Key: "this app only" = specific form, not All Forms
________________________________________
Q143: Which actions work in Canvas app business rules?
Answer: ✅ A (Validate + error), C (Set required), D (Set values)
❌ B (Enable/disable) — NOT available in canvas
❌ E (Show/hide) — NOT available in canvas
________________________________________
Q157-158: Farm canvas app — business rule across multiple forms
Answer: ✅ A. Table
Why: Canvas app = always Table scope. Even though the question mentions "several forms", canvas apps use Table scope
❌ All Forms — model-driven only
❌ Form specific — model-driven only
________________________________________
Q89-90: Business rule to prevent duplicates?
Answer: ❌ No — Business rules cannot check for duplicates across records. They only work on the current record's form
Use instead: Duplicate Detection Rules or Alternate Keys
________________________________________
Q95: Business rule components (lock, trigger, recommendations)
•	Lock a field → Lock/Unlock action
•	Trigger logic based on field value → Condition
•	Use existing data to enhance entry → Recommendation
Key: Recommendations are available in model-driven apps only (not canvas)
________________________________________
🔧 Apply Section — Verify It Yourself
✅ Exercise 1: See the Scope Dropdown
1.	make.powerapps.com → Tables → Account → Business Rules
2.	Open any business rule (or create new)
3.	Look at the top-right corner → you'll see a Scope dropdown
4.	Click it → see options: Table, All Forms, or specific form names
5.	Change it and observe: 
o	Table → rule shows "(Table)" in the list
o	All Forms → rule shows "(All Forms)"
✅ Exercise 2: Test Table Scope in Canvas App
1.	Create a business rule on Account table with Scope = Table
2.	Action: If "Account Name" contains "TEST" → set "Description" to "Test Account"
3.	Save & Activate
4.	Open a Canvas app connected to Account table
5.	Create a record with name "TEST" → verify Description auto-fills
6.	Now change scope to All Forms → repeat → it WON'T work in canvas app
✅ Exercise 3: Test Specific Form Scope
1.	Create a business rule with Scope = specific form (e.g., "Account Main Form")
2.	Open that form in a model-driven app → rule works ✅
3.	Open a DIFFERENT form for the same table → rule does NOT apply ❌
4.	This proves: specific form scope = only that one form
✅ Exercise 4: Canvas App Limitations
1.	Create a business rule with Scope = Table
2.	Try adding a "Show/Hide" action → you CAN add it in the designer
3.	BUT: Open a canvas app → the show/hide action is IGNORED
4.	Only validate, set value, and set required actually work in canvas apps
